  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front will continue moving through the area \nthis afternoon and be offshore by early this evening resulting in \ndry but breezy conditions. \n\nA modest northwest pressure gradient is setting up as high pressure \ncurrently centered to our west builds towards the area in the wake \nof a cold front. Generally expect a clearing trend in skies this \nafternoon with NW winds 10-15 gusting 20 to 30 mph. Afternoon highs \nshould mainly be in the upper 60s to low 70s. \n\nTonight will be by far the coolest so far this season, as \ntemperatures fall into the 40s in many areas by dawn Tuesday. \nCoastal areas and the Philly urban core will likely stay closer to \n50 degrees, and areas north of I-78 will drop into the mid to upper \n30s in some spots. There could be some patchy frost in the sheltered \nvalleys of NW NJ and the southern Poconos, but confidence on this is \nsomewhat low give the gradient wind that will still be in place. \nThis should help prevent strong radiational cooling and thus \ntemperatures from bottoming out too much.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Tranquil weather will prevail this week continuing \ninto the weekend with no significant weather hazards anticipated. \n\nHigh pressure will be in control through much of the week resulting \nin mostly clear/sunny skies and seasonably cool to mild \ntemperatures. The first notably cool Canadian airmass of the season \nwill settle into the area by Tuesday. Highs on Tuesday will only get \ninto the low to mid 60s, with overnight lows Tuesday night/Wednesday \nmorning dropping into the upper 30s and low to mid 40s.\n\nForecast temperatures are high enough to not need any frost \nheadlines just yet. The radiational cooling setup Tuesday night is \nnot the most ideal. While surface winds will be light to calm, winds \nnot far above the surface will be modest and beginning to advect \nhigher moisture air that is closer to saturation aloft into the \narea. This combined with rebounding dewpoints should work to \nsuppress strong radiational cooling despite mostly clear skies and \nlight surface winds.Nevertheless, there could certainly be at least \nsome patchy frost in the sheltered valleys of northwest New Jersey \nand the southern Poconos, where temperatures could locally drop into \nthe mid 30s.\n\nAs high pressure shifts offshore by Wednesday, airmass modification \nwill commence. Temperatures return to more seasonable values on \nWednesday, and likely go above normal on Thursday with highs in the \nmid to upper 70s. Temperatures look to return to near normal again \nfor Friday and into the weekend. It should be an extended stretch of \npleasant weather days with the next chance for rain not arriving \nuntil Sunday at the earliest.",
